options.explanation:
Saying "$y$ is an implicit function of $x$" means the same as saying that $y(x)$ is a function whose equation is unknown.

The derivative of $y(x)$ is $y'(x)$, or $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.

We treat any expression that contains $y$ as a composite function, so differentiating it involves the chain rule.

For example, this is how we differentiate the term $-y^2$ (from our equation) implicitly:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}(-y^2)&=-\dfrac{d}{dx}[y(x)]^2&&\gray{\text{Write }y\text{ as }y(x)}
\\\\
&=-2y(x)\cdot y'(x)&&\gray{\text{Chain rule}}
\\\\
&=-2y\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}&&\gray{\text{Go back to }y\text{ and }\dfrac{dy}{dx}}
\end{align}$

hints.content:
Now we can plug the point $(2,4)$ into the expression for $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=-\dfrac{1+2y}{2x-2y}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{1+2(4)}{2(2)-2(4)}\qquad\gray{x=2,\,\,y=4}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{9}{-4}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{9}{4}
\end{align}$
